#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# Simplification and assumptions

#% text
# `simplify` and `full_simplify` look for a simpler form of an expression, `expand` and `factor` give the two standard shapes of a polynomial, and `assuming` tells them facts about the symbols (a variable is positive, an integer) so that more identities hold.

#% section
# Choosing a form

#% cell
expand((x + 1)^4)
#% output
#= 1 + 4*x + 6*x^2 + 4*x^3 + x^4

#% cell
factor(x^4 - 1)
#% output
#= (-1 + x)*(1 + x)*(1 + x^2)

#% cell
simplify((x^2 - 1)/(x - 1))
#% output
#= 1 + x

#% cell
simplify(sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
simplify(sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2 + gamma(x + 1)/gamma(x))
#% output
#= 1 + x

#% text
# `simplify` has no options: it picks the form with the fewest leaves, and already applies many identities of special functions. `full_simplify` is the wider search: it also tries more expansions and identities between functions, and may take longer. On these two examples they agree; try `full_simplify` when `simplify` leaves a form you do not like.

#% cell
[simplify(x*gamma(x)), full_simplify(x*gamma(x))]
#% output
#= [gamma(1 + x), gamma(1 + x)]

#% cell
e = csc(pi*v)*(bessel_i(-v, z) - bessel_i(v, z))/2; [simplify(e), full_simplify(e)]
#% output
#= [bessel_k(v, z)/pi, bessel_k(v, z)/pi]

#% section
# Assumptions

#% text
# Many identities hold only for some values: `sqrt(x^2)` is `x` only when `x` is not negative. Without a fact the engine leaves it alone.

#% cell
simplify(sqrt(x^2))
#% output
#= (x^2)^(1/2)

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, simplify(sqrt(x^2)))
#% output
#= x

#% cell
assuming(element(n, integers), simplify(sin(n*pi)))
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
refine(abs(x), x > 0)
#% output
#= x

#% cell
refine(abs(x), x < 0)
#% output
#= -x

#% text
# `assuming(facts, body)` adds the facts for the evaluation of `body`; `refine(e, facts)` rewrites a single expression.

#% section
# Special functions

#% cell
function_expand(gamma(n + 3)/gamma(n + 1))
#% output
#= (1 + n)*(2 + n)

#% cell
function_expand(bessel_j(1/2, x))
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*sin(x)/(pi*x)^(1/2)

#% section
# Common mistakes

#% item
# `simplify` does not guess a domain: `sqrt(x^2)` stays until you say something about `x`.

#% item
# Equal expressions can have different shapes; "simplest" is a choice. Compare the forms of one polynomial.

#% cell
[expand((x - 1)*(x + 1)), factor(x^2 - 1)]
#% output
#= [-1 + x^2, (-1 + x)*(1 + x)]

#% item
# Assumptions apply only inside `assuming` (or in a `refine` call); they do not stay after it.

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, refine(abs(x))); refine(abs(x))
#% output
#= abs(x)

#% section
# Related functions

#% text
# `simplify`, `full_simplify`, `expand`, `factor`, `assuming`, `refine`, `function_expand`
